# p5 mcp: c1db919's /mcp menu with the same three servers as
# p5-mcp-menu-new: its sections, each server's details, help, the
# project-trust confirmation, each left with Esc.
# The stdio server is p5-mcp-stdio.ts, which the profile names by `@BUN@` and
# `@SCENARIOS@` (capture.sh writes both in), and xfx is given the same profile.
work p5-mcp-ws
fx: homefile .fx/mcp.json p5-mcp-profile.json
xfx: homefile .xfx/mcp.json p5-mcp-profile.json
wait 4
snap startup
key Escape
wait 1
snap dismissed
type /mcp
key Enter
wait 2
snap list
key Down
wait 0.5
snap list-down
key Tab
wait 1
snap tools
key Tab
wait 1
snap resources
key Tab
wait 1
snap prompts
key Tab
wait 1
snap servers
key Enter
wait 1
snap details-1
key Escape
wait 0.5
key Down
wait 0.3
key Enter
wait 1
snap details-2
key Escape
wait 0.5
key Down
wait 0.3
key Enter
wait 1
snap details-3
key Escape
wait 0.5
type c
wait 0.7
snap info
key Escape
wait 0.5
type x
wait 0.7
snap x-key
key Escape
wait 0.5
type d
wait 0.7
snap d-key
# The menus are closed by now (x and d went to the draft, `xd`). An Escape
# at a prompt with a draft arms `esc again to clear` for 500 ms on both
# binaries (xfx src/tui/gesture.rs CLEAR_WINDOW, fx
# gesture_state.zig:4@481104fd): a second Escape inside it clears the
# draft, a snap after it sees the row gone on xfx and still up on fx (fx
# showed it 0.6 s after the Escape). This ending used to time both with
# `wait 0.5`, on the window's edge each time (LAUNCH-SETTLE). Now it
# waits for the row, takes the draft away a character at a time, and
# waits for the row to go (fx takes it down at the Backspace, xfx when its
# 500 ms run out): `closed` and `end` read an empty prompt with no timer
# up.
key Escape
until shown esc again to clear
key BSpace
key BSpace
until gone esc again to clear
snap closed
snapfull end
home end
